Mitigate starved-gesture flings, and instrument the touch path that causes them.

Follow-up to webkit-kinetic-fling-velocity-fixes.patch, whose end-event repair did
NOT fix the dead flings: franceinfo.fr still lost 2 of 10 flicks after it landed
(3 of 8 before). The diagnostic explains why - the degenerate case reports
motion=1, not motion=0:

  DEGENERATE samples=2 motion=1 span=0ms -> velocity 0,0 (NO MOMENTUM)

The history was never being over-pruned. During post-load churn the entire flick
arrives as a SINGLE motion wheel event instead of the usual 10-12, and a milder
form shows the same starvation (motion=2, meanInterval=144ms -> 2500 px/s against
a 4400 px/s norm). With one sample there is no interval to divide by, so no
velocity is computable at any layer of the estimator. The loss happens upstream,
in touch event delivery, when the main thread is saturated.

Two changes, kept deliberately separate:

1. MITIGATION (WEBKIT_KINETIC_SINGLE_SAMPLE_FALLBACK, default on): when exactly
   one motion sample survives, charge its delta one nominal frame interval
   (WEBKIT_KINETIC_NOMINAL_FRAME_MS, default 16.7) instead of returning zero, so
   the flick produces a short fling rather than stopping dead. Clamped by
   WEBKIT_KINETIC_MAX_VELOCITY, because a starved gesture can deliver the whole
   flick distance in one event and 1200px/16ms would imply an absurd speed.
   This papers over the starvation; it does not fix it.

2. INSTRUMENTATION ([touchgesture], WEBKIT_FLINGVEL_LOG=1): count raw touch
   motion events entering TouchGestureController against synthesized axis events
   leaving it, per gesture. This localises the loss: "in" already ~1 means the
   touch events never reached this layer and the repair belongs further down
   (delivery/ack-timeout); healthy "in" with "out"=1 means the loss is here.

Also bounds a wart introduced by the previous patch: zero-delta end events age
out nothing, so a run of starved gestures left one stale end event behind per
flick (visible as samples=2,3,4 while motion stayed 1). They are now collapsed
to the newest.

Must apply AFTER webkit-kinetic-fling-velocity-fixes.patch.

diff -ruN a/Source/WebCore/platform/ScrollAnimationKinetic.cpp b/Source/WebCore/platform/ScrollAnimationKinetic.cpp
--- a/Source/WebCore/platform/ScrollAnimationKinetic.cpp	2026-07-20 20:00:15.785246791 +0000
+++ b/Source/WebCore/platform/ScrollAnimationKinetic.cpp	2026-07-20 20:00:49.739457581 +0000
@@ -196,6 +196,32 @@
     return value;
 }
 
+// Mitigation for starved gestures that deliver a single motion sample: charge that
+// lone delta one nominal frame interval rather than reporting zero velocity.
+// WEBKIT_KINETIC_SINGLE_SAMPLE_FALLBACK=0 disables it (restoring the dead fling);
+// WEBKIT_KINETIC_NOMINAL_FRAME_MS sets the assumed interval (default 16.7ms).
+static bool kineticSingleSampleFallbackEnabled()
+{
+    static bool enabled = [] {
+        const char* v = getenv("WEBKIT_KINETIC_SINGLE_SAMPLE_FALLBACK");
+        return !v || (*v && *v != '0');
+    }();
+    return enabled;
+}
+
+static double nominalFrameIntervalSeconds()
+{
+    static double value = [] {
+        if (const char* env = getenv("WEBKIT_KINETIC_NOMINAL_FRAME_MS")) {
+            double v = atof(env);
+            if (v >= 1.0 && v <= 200.0)
+                return v / 1000.0;
+        }
+        return 1.0 / 60.0;
+    }();
+    return value;
+}
+
 // Absolute ceiling on accumulated fling velocity, in px/s. 0 disables the clamp.
 // WEBKIT_KINETIC_MAX_VELOCITY tunes it.
 static double kineticMaxVelocity()
@@ -214,6 +240,12 @@
 void ScrollAnimationKinetic::appendToScrollHistory(const PlatformWheelEvent& event)
 {
     if (kineticEndEventFixEnabled() && !wheelEventHasMotion(event)) {
+        // Keep the end event (its timestamp still bounds the gesture) but never let
+        // zero-delta events pile up: they age out nothing, so without this a run of
+        // starved gestures leaves one stale end event behind per flick.
+        m_scrollHistory.removeAllMatching([] (PlatformWheelEvent& otherEvent) -> bool {
+            return !wheelEventHasMotion(otherEvent);
+        });
         m_scrollHistory.append(event);
         return;
     }
@@ -265,18 +297,50 @@
     auto first = kineticEndEventFixEnabled() && firstMotion ? *firstMotion : m_scrollHistory[0].timestamp();
     auto last = kineticEndEventFixEnabled() && lastMotion ? *lastMotion : m_scrollHistory.rbegin()->timestamp();
 
-    if (last == first) {
-        if (flingVelLogEnabled())
-            fprintf(stderr, "[flingvel] DEGENERATE samples=%zu motion=%zu span=0ms -> velocity 0,0 (NO MOMENTUM)\n", m_scrollHistory.size(), motionCount);
-        return { };
-    }
-
     FloatPoint accumDelta;
     for (const auto& scrollEvent : m_scrollHistory)
         accumDelta += FloatPoint(scrollEvent.deltaX(), scrollEvent.deltaY());
 
     size_t samples = m_scrollHistory.size();
 
+    if (last == first) {
+        // Only one motion sample reached us, so there is no interval to divide by.
+        // This is not an arithmetic failure: on a busy page the touch->wheel motion
+        // events starve and the whole flick arrives as a single sample (measured on
+        // franceinfo.fr during post-load churn). Returning {0,0} makes the page stop
+        // dead under the finger.
+        //
+        // MITIGATION, not a fix: charge the lone delta one nominal frame interval so
+        // the gesture produces *a* fling instead of nothing. The real repair belongs
+        // in touch event delivery - see the [touchgesture] counters. The estimate is
+        // deliberately clamped, because a starved gesture can deliver the entire
+        // flick distance in one event, which over 16ms would imply an absurd speed.
+        double singleSampleVelocityY = 0;
+        double singleSampleVelocityX = 0;
+        if (kineticSingleSampleFallbackEnabled() && motionCount == 1) {
+            double interval = nominalFrameIntervalSeconds();
+            singleSampleVelocityX = accumDelta.x() * -1 / interval;
+            singleSampleVelocityY = accumDelta.y() * -1 / interval;
+            double ceiling = kineticMaxVelocity();
+            if (ceiling > 0) {
+                if (std::abs(singleSampleVelocityX) > ceiling)
+                    singleSampleVelocityX = std::copysign(ceiling, singleSampleVelocityX);
+                if (std::abs(singleSampleVelocityY) > ceiling)
+                    singleSampleVelocityY = std::copysign(ceiling, singleSampleVelocityY);
+            }
+        }
+
+        if (flingVelLogEnabled()) {
+            fprintf(stderr, "[flingvel] SINGLE-SAMPLE samples=%zu motion=%zu span=0ms accumDelta=%.1f,%.1f -> fallback velocity=%.0f,%.0f px/s%s\n",
+                m_scrollHistory.size(), motionCount, accumDelta.x(), accumDelta.y(),
+                singleSampleVelocityX, singleSampleVelocityY,
+                singleSampleVelocityY ? "" : " (NO MOMENTUM)");
+        }
+
+        m_scrollHistory.clear();
+        return FloatSize(singleSampleVelocityX, singleSampleVelocityY);
+    }
+
     // FIXME: It's odd that computeVelocity() has the side effect of clearing history.
     m_scrollHistory.clear();
 
